# 2. Binary Search

Binary Search is an efficient searching technique that works on a sorted or appropriately structured search space. It repeatedly divides the search space into two halves, giving an `O(log n)` search time in the standard case.

| Sr. No. | Problem Title                                                   | Problem Statement                                                                                                                                                                              | LeetCode Link                                                                                                                        |
| ------: | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
|      13 | **Basic Binary Search**                                         | Given a sorted array of integers and a target value, find the index of the target using Binary Search. Return `-1` if the target is not present.                                               | [LeetCode 704 – Binary Search](https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search/)                                                         |
|      14 | **Search Insert Position**                                      | Given a sorted array of distinct integers and a target value, return the index if the target exists. Otherwise, return the index where the target should be inserted to maintain sorted order. | [LeetCode 35 – Search Insert Position](https://leetcode.com/problems/search-insert-position/)                                        |
|      15 | **First Occurrence of Target**                                  | Given a sorted array that may contain duplicate values and a target, find the index of the first occurrence of the target. Return `-1` if it is not present.                                   | [LeetCode 34 – Find First and Last Position](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array/) |
|      16 | **Last Occurrence of Target**                                   | Given a sorted array that may contain duplicate values and a target, find the index of the last occurrence of the target. Return `-1` if it is not present.                                    | [LeetCode 34 – Find First and Last Position](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array/) |
|      17 | **First Missing Element in Sorted Array of 1 to N**             | Given a sorted array containing consecutive numbers with one or more missing values, use Binary Search to identify the first missing element.                                                  | [LeetCode 268 – Missing Number](https://leetcode.com/problems/missing-number/)                                                       |
|      18 | **First Positive Element in Sorted Array**                      | Given a sorted array containing negative, zero, and positive values, find the index of the first positive element using Binary Search.                                                         | —                                                                                                                                    |
|      19 | **First Element Greater Than or Equal to Target (Upper Bound)** | Given a sorted array and a target value, find the first element whose value is greater than or equal to the target.                                                                            | [LeetCode 744 – Find Smallest Letter Greater Than Target](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-smallest-letter-greater-than-target/)   |
|      20 | **Lower Bound**                                                 | Given a sorted array and a target value, find the first position where an element is greater than or equal to the target. If no such element exists, return the size of the array.             | [LeetCode 35 – Search Insert Position](https://leetcode.com/problems/search-insert-position/)                                        |
|      21 | **Peak in Mountain Array**                                      | Given a mountain array that strictly increases and then strictly decreases, find the index of the peak element.                                                                                | [LeetCode 852 – Peak Index in a Mountain Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/peak-index-in-a-mountain-array/)                       |
|      22 | **Local Peak in Unsorted Array**                                | Given an unsorted array, find an index of a peak element. A peak element is an element that is greater than its neighboring elements.                                                          | [LeetCode 162 – Find Peak Element](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-peak-element/)                                                 |
|      23 | **Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array**                             | Given a sorted array that has been rotated an unknown number of times, find the minimum element using Binary Search.                                                                           | [LeetCode 153 – Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array/)           |
|      24 | **Search in Rotated Sorted Array**                              | Given a rotated sorted array and a target value, search for the target and return its index. Return `-1` if the target does not exist.                                                         | [LeetCode 33 – Search in Rotated Sorted Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/search-in-rotated-sorted-array/)                        |

**Note:** In standard Binary Search terminology, **Lower Bound** is the first position containing a value `>= target`. The LeetCode Search Insert Position problem implements this same first-valid-position pattern.

